Problem:
The points $(x, y)$ represented in this table lie on a straight line. The point $(13, q)$ lies on the same line. What is the value of $p + q?$ Express your answer as a decimal to the nearest tenth. $$\begin{array}{c|c}
x & y \\ \hline
2 & -5 \\
p & -14 \\
p+2 & -17 \\
\end{array}$$

Solution:
answer:  We can find the slope of the line using the first two points:

$$\frac{-14 - (-5)}{p - 2} = \frac{-9}{p-2} = -1.$$

Since the slope is constant, we can use the third point to find $p$:

$$\frac{-17 - (-14)}{p+2 - p} = \frac{-3}{2} = -1.$$

Solving for $p$, we get:

$$p = 7.$$

Now, we can use the slope to find $q$ for the point $(13, q)$:

$$\frac{q - (-5)}{13 - 2} = \frac{q + 5}{11} = -1.$$

Solving for $q$, we get:

$$q = -6.$$

Therefore, $p + q = 7 + (-6) = \boxed{1}$.
