Problem:
Solve for $x$: $$(\sqrt{12x}+12)(\sqrt{3x}-6)=4(x+3)+x-34$$

Solution:
answer:  First, simplify the left-hand side of the equation:

$$(\sqrt{12x}+12)(\sqrt{3x}-6) = 12x - 72$$

Next, simplify the right-hand side of the equation:

$$4(x+3) + x - 34 = 4x + 12 + x - 34 = 5x - 22$$

Now, set the two expressions equal to each other:

$$12x - 72 = 5x - 22$$

Subtract $5x$ from both sides:

$$7x - 72 = -22$$

Add 72 to both sides:

$$7x = 50$$

Divide by 7:

$$x = \frac{50}{7} = \boxed{7}$$
====================

====================
