Problem:
If $2x - 9y = 14$ and $6x = 42 +y$, what is the value of the product $xy$?

Solution:
answer:  First, we solve the system of equations:

\begin{align*}
2x - 9y &= 14 \\
6x &= 42 + y
\end{align*}

Subtract the first equation from the second:

\begin{align*}
6x - (2x - 9y) &= 42 + y - 14 \\
8y &= 28 \\
y &= 3
\end{align*}

Now, substitute $y = 3$ back into the first equation:

\begin{align*}
2x - 9(3) &= 14 \\
2x - 27 &= 14 \\
2x &= 41 \\
x &= 20.5
\end{align*}

Now, we find the product $xy$:

\begin{align*}
xy &= 20.5 \cdot 3 \\
xy &= 61.5
\end{align*}

Therefore, the value of the product $xy$ is $\boxed{61.5}$.
