Problem:
If $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are both seventh-degree polynomials in $t$, what is the degree of $p(t)\cdot q(t)$?

Solution:
answer:  To determine the degree of the product \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \), we need to consider the degrees of the individual polynomials. Given that both \( p(t) \) and \( q(t) \) are seventh-degree polynomials, we can use the properties of polynomial multiplication.

The degree of the product of two polynomials is the sum of the degrees of the polynomials. Therefore, the degree of \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \) is:

\[
\text{Degree of } p(t) \cdot q(t) = \text{Degree of } p(t) + \text{Degree of } q(t) = 7 + 7 = 14
\]

Thus, the degree of \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \) is \(\boxed{14}\).
