Problem:
For some constants $a$ and $b,$ let \[f(x) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
9 - 2x & \text{if } x \le 3, \\
ax + b & \text{if } x > 3.
\end{array}
\right.\]The function $f$ has the property that $f(f(x)) = x$ for all $x.$ What is $a + b?$

Solution:
answer:  For $x \le 3,$ $f(f(x)) = f(9 - 2x) = 9 - 2(9 - 2x) = x.$

For $x > 3,$ $f(f(x)) = f(ax + b) = a(ax + b) + b = ax^2 + ab + b.$

Since $f(f(x)) = x$ for all $x,$ we have $ax^2 + ab + b = x$ for all $x > 3.$

Therefore, $a = 1$ and $b = 3.$

Thus, $a + b = \boxed{4}.$
