Problem:
My usual workout consists of running 10 laps around a 400 meter track. If I go to a track that is only 250 meters long, how many laps will I need to run to get the same workout?

Solution:
answer:  To determine how many laps you need to run on the 250-meter track to get the same workout, we first need to calculate the total distance you run in your usual workout.

If you run 10 laps around a 400-meter track, the total distance is:
\[ 10 \times{ laps} \times 400 \text{ meters/lap} = 4000 \text{ meters} \]

Next, we need to find out how many laps you need to run on the 250-meter track to cover 4000 meters. We do this by dividing the total distance by the length of the new track:
\[ \frac{4000 \text{ meters}}{250 \text{ meters/lap}} = 16 \text{ laps} \]

Therefore, you will need to run 16 laps on the 250-meter track to get the same workout.

Final Answer: The final answer is $16$. I hope it is correct.
