Problem:
Expand and simplify completely: \begin{align*}
x\left(x(1+x)+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

Solution:
answer:  First, expand the expression inside the parentheses:
\begin{align*}
x\left(x(1+x)+2x\right) &= x(x^2 + x) + 2x \\
&= x^3 + x^2 + 2x \\
&= x^3 + x^2 + 2x.
\end{align*}

Next, distribute the $-3$ in the parentheses:
\begin{align*}
-3(x^2 - x + 2) &= -3x^2 + 3x - 6.
\end{align*}

Now, combine the two expressions:
\begin{align*}
x^3 + x^2 + 2x - 3x^2 + 3x - 6 &= x^3 - 2x^2 + x - 6.
\end{align*}

Therefore, the simplified expression is:
\boxed{x^3 - 2x^2 + x - 6}.
