Problem:
When the first $50$ counting numbers are arranged in columns, as shown, what is the sum of all of the counting numbers in column $\text E?$ \begin{tabular}{lllll}
A & B & C & D & E\\
1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5\\
10 & 9 & 8 & 7 & 6\\
11 & 12 & 13 & 14 & 15\\
20 & 19 & 18 & 17 & 16\\
. & . & . & . & .\\
. & . & . & . & .\\
. & . & . & . & .
\end{tabular}

Solution:
answer:  To find the sum of all the counting numbers in column E, we need to identify the pattern in the column. Column E consists of the numbers 5, 6, 15, 16, ..., 50. This is an arithmetic sequence where the first term is 5 and the common difference is 1.

The number of terms in this sequence can be found by dividing the last term by the first term by the common difference and adding 1:
\[
\frac{50 - 5}{1} + 1 = 46
\]

The sum of the first \( n \) terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by the formula:
\[
S = \frac{n}{2} \times (\text{first term} + \text{last term})
\]

Substituting the given values:
\[
S = \frac{46}{2} \times (5 + 50) = 23 \times 55 = 1215
\]

Thus, the sum of all the counting numbers in column E is:
\[
\boxed{1215}
\]
