Problem:
Simplify and rationalize the denominator: $$\frac{8}{3\sqrt{3}+ 7\sqrt{27}}.$$

Solution:
answer:  We can simplify the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:

$$\frac{8}{3\sqrt{3}+ 7\sqrt{27}} \cdot \frac{3\sqrt{3}- 7\sqrt{27}}{3\sqrt{3}- 7\sqrt{27}} = \frac{8(3\sqrt{3}- 7\sqrt{27})}{(3\sqrt{3}+ 7\sqrt{27})(3\sqrt{3}- 7\sqrt{27})} = \frac{8(3\sqrt{3}- 7\sqrt{27})}{27-927} = \frac{8(3\sqrt{3}- 7\sqrt{27})}{-900} = -\frac{3\sqrt{3}- 7\sqrt{27}}{125}.$$

Therefore, the simplified and rationalized form of the expression is $\boxed{-\frac{3\sqrt{3}- 7\sqrt{27}}{125}}$.
