Problem:
If $(7,9)$ and $(10,2)$ are the coordinates of two opposite vertices of a square, what is the sum of the $y$-coordinates of the other two vertices?

Solution:
answer:  Since the vertices of a square are negatives of each other, the coordinates of the other two vertices will be the negatives of the given coordinates. Therefore, the coordinates of the other two vertices are $(-7,-9)$ and $(-10,-2)$. 

The sum of the $y$-coordinates of these two vertices is $-9 + (-2) = \boxed{-11}$.
