Problem:
If \begin{align*}
2x-y&=3,\\
x+y &=1,
\end{align*}compute $8x-7y$.

Solution:
answer:  We can solve the system of equations by adding the two equations together:

\begin{align*}
(2x-y)+(x+y)&=3+1\\
3x&=4\\
x&=1.
\end{align*}

Substituting $x=1$ into the second equation, we get:

\begin{align*}
1+y&=1\\
y&=0.
\end{align*}

Now, we can compute $8x-7y$:

\begin{align*}
8x-7y&=8(1)-7(0)\\
&=8-0\\
&=\boxed{8}.
\end{align*}
