Problem:
Expand $(z^2 -3z+2)(z^3 + 4z-2).$

Solution:
answer:  We can use the distributive property to expand the expression:

\begin{align*}
(z^2 - 3z + 2)(z^3 + 4z - 2) &= z^2(z^3 + 4z - 2) - 3z(z^3 + 4z - 2) + 2(z^3 + 4z - 2) \\
&= z^5 + 4z^3 - 2z^2 - 3z^4 - 12z^2 + 6z + 2z^3 + 8z - 4 \\
&= z^5 - 3z^4 - 10z^3 - 14z^2 + 14z - 4.
\end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $z^5 - 3z^4 - 10z^3 - 14z^2 + 14z - 4$. I hope it is correct.
