Problem:
Suppose a function $f(x)$ has domain $(-\infty,\infty)$ and range $[-11,3]$. If we define a new function $g(x)$ by $$g(x) = f(6x)+1,$$ then what is the range of $g(x)$? Express your answer in interval notation.

Solution:
answer:  To determine the range of the function \( g(x) = f(6x) + 1 \), we start by analyzing the range of the function \( f(x) \). The range of \( f(x) \) is given as \([-11, 3]\).

Next, we consider the transformation \( g(x) = f(6x) + 1 \). This transformation shifts the graph of \( f(x) \) by 1 unit upward. Therefore, the range of \( g(x) \) will be the same as the range of \( f(x) \) shifted upward by 1 unit.

The range of \( f(x) \) is \([-11, 3]\). Shifting this range upward by 1 unit results in the range \([-10, 3 + 1] = [-10, 4]\).

Thus, the range of \( g(x) \) is \(\boxed{[-10, 4]}\).
