Problem:
If $(2^x-2)(5\cdot2^x+10)=3\cdot4^x+12$, what is $x$?

Solution:
answer:  We can rewrite the equation as

$$(2^x-2)(5\cdot2^x+10)=3\cdot4^x+12.$$

Since $4^x=(2^{2x})^x$, we have

$$(2^x-2)(5\cdot2^x+10)=3\cdot2^{2x}+12.$$

Expanding and simplifying, we get

$$10\cdot2^x-20=3\cdot2^{2x}+12.$$

Rearranging terms, we have

$$10\cdot2^x-3\cdot2^{2x}=12+20.$$

Simplifying, we get

$$10\cdot2^x-3\cdot2^{2x}=32.$$

Factoring out $2^x$, we have

$$2^x(10-3\cdot2^x)=32.$$

Since $2^x$ is nonzero, we can divide both sides by $2^x$:

$$10-3\cdot2^x=0.$$

Solving for $x$, we get

$$x=\boxed{2}.$$
====================

