Problem:
For complex numbers $z$, let \[f(z) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
z^{2}&\text{ if }z\text{ is not real}, \\
z+2 &\text{ if }z\text{ is real}.
\end{array}
\right.\]Find $f(i)+f(1)+f(-1)+f(-i)$.

Solution:
answer:  We have that $f(i) = i^2 = -1$, $f(1) = 1+2 = 3$, $f(-1) = -1+2 = 1$, and $f(-i) = (-i)^2 = -1$. Therefore, $f(i) + f(1) + f(-1) + f(-i) = -1 + 3 + 1 + (-1) = \boxed{2}$.
====================

