Problem:
What is the greatest integer value of $x$ for which $6x^2 + x - 2 < 0$?

Solution:
answer:  We can factor the quadratic expression $6x^2 + x - 2$ as $(6x - 1)(x + 2) < 0$. The roots of the equation are $x = \frac{1}{6}$ and $x = -2$. We can use a sign chart to determine the sign of the expression in each interval defined by the roots.

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
 & (-\infty, -2) & (-2, \frac{1}{6}) & (\frac{1}{6}, \infty) \\
\hline
6x - 1 & - & - & + \\
x + 2 & - & - & + \\
\hline
6x^2 + x - 2 & + & - & + \\
\end{array}
\]

The expression $6x^2 + x - 2$ is negative in the interval $(-2, \frac{1}{6})$. The greatest integer value of $x$ in this interval is $x = -1$.

\boxed{-1}
