Problem:
Ben rolls two fair six-sided dice. What is the expected value of the larger of the two numbers rolled? Express your answer as a fraction.  (If the two numbers are the same, we take that number to be the "larger" number.)

Solution:
answer:  We can calculate the expected value of the larger of the two numbers rolled by considering the possible outcomes and their probabilities.

There are 36 possible outcomes when rolling two six-sided dice. The probability of each outcome is $\frac{1}{36}$.

The expected value of the larger of the two numbers rolled can be calculated as follows:

$$E(\text{larger}) = \frac{1}{36} \sum_{i=1}^{6} \sum_{j=1}^{6} \max(i, j)$$

We can simplify this calculation by considering the possible outcomes and their probabilities:

$$E(\text{larger}) = \frac{1}{36} \sum_{i=1}^{6} \sum_{j=1}^{6} \max(i, j)$$

$$E(\text{larger}) = \frac{1}{36} \sum_{i=1}^{6} \sum_{j=1}^{6} \max(i, j)$$

$$E(\text{larger}) = \frac{7}{2}$$

Final Answer: The final answer is $\frac{7}{2}$. I hope it is correct.
