Problem:
There are 190 people on the beach.  110 are wearing sunglasses, 70 are wearing bathing suits, and 95 are wearing a hat.  Everyone is wearing at least one of these items. 30 are wearing both bathing suits and sunglasses. 25 are wearing both bathing suits and a hat. 40 are wearing both sunglasses and a hat.  How many people are wearing all three items?

Solution:
answer:  We can use the principle of inclusion-exclusion to find the number of people wearing all three items. Let $S$ be the set of people wearing sunglasses, $B$ be the set of people wearing bathing suits, and $H$ be the set of people wearing a hat. We have:

$$|S \cup B \cup H| = |S| + |B| + |H| - |S \cap B| - |B \cap H| - |S \cap H| + |S \cap B \cap H|$$

Substituting the given values, we get:

$$190 = 110 + 70 + 95 - 30 - 25 - 40 + |S \cap B \cap H|$$

Simplifying, we get:

$$190 = 180 + |S \cap B \cap H|$$

Solving for $|S \cap B \cap H|$, we get:

$$|S \cap B \cap H| = \boxed{10}$$

Final Answer: The final answer is $10$. I hope it is correct.
