Problem:
The point $A(3,3)$ is reflected across the $x$-axis to $A^{'}$. Then $A^{'}$ is translated two units to the left to $A^{''}$. The coordinates of $A^{''}$ are $(x,y)$. What is the value of $x+y$? [asy]
draw((-7,0)--(7,0),linewidth(1),Arrows);
draw((0,-7)--(0,7),linewidth(1),Arrows);

for(int i=-5; i<0; ++i)
{

draw((i,-.5)--(i,.5),linewidth(1));
}

for(int i=1; i<6; ++i)
{

draw((i,-.5)--(i,.5),linewidth(1));
}

for(int i=-5; i<0; ++i)
{

draw((-.5,i)--(.5,i),linewidth(1));
}

for(int i=-5; i<0; ++i)
{

draw((-.5,-i)--(.5,-i),linewidth(1));
}

label("$x$", (8,0));
label("$y$", (0,8));

[/asy]

Solution:
answer:  First, we reflect point $A(3,3)$ across the $x$-axis to get $A'(-3,3)$. Then, we translate $A'$ two units to the left to get $A''(-5,3)$. Therefore, the coordinates of $A''$ are $(-5,3)$, and $x+y=-5+3=\boxed{-2}$.
