Problem:
Given regular pentagon $ABCDE,$ a circle can be drawn that is tangent to $\overline{DC}$ at $D$ and to $\overline{AB}$ at $A.$ In degrees, what is the measure of minor arc $AD$? [asy]import olympiad; import geometry; size(100); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8));
pair[] pentagon = new pair[5];
pentagon[0] = dir(36);
pentagon.cyclic=true;
for(int i = 1; i < 6; ++i){
 
pentagon[i] = dir(72*i + 36);

draw(pentagon[i - 1]--pentagon[i]);
}
dot("$C$",pentagon[0],NE);
dot("$D$",pentagon[1],N);
dot("$E$",pentagon[2],W);
dot("$A$",pentagon[3],S);
dot("$B$",pentagon[4],SE);
line x = line(pentagon[1],pentagon[1] + dir(-108));
line x2 = line(origin,pentagon[2]);
pair ccenter = coordinates(intersectionpoint(x,x2));
draw(Circle(ccenter,arclength(ccenter--pentagon[1])));
[/asy]

Solution:
answer:  First, we note that the regular pentagon $ABCDE$ has an interior angle of $108^\circ$. The circle is tangent to $\overline{DC}$ at $D$ and to $\overline{AB}$ at $A$, which means that the circle is tangent to the sides of the pentagon at these points.

Since the circle is tangent to the sides of the pentagon, the radius of the circle is equal to the length of the side of the pentagon. The center of the circle is the midpoint of the segments $AD$ and $DC$.

The measure of minor arc $AD$ is the same as the measure of the central angle $AD$. Since the circle is tangent to the sides of the pentagon, the central angle $AD$ is equal to the interior angle of the pentagon, which is $108^\circ$.

Therefore, the measure of minor arc $AD$ is $\boxed{108^\circ}$.
