Problem:
In the diagram, $PRT$ and $QRS$ are straight lines. What is the value of $x$? [asy]
import olympiad;
import math;

size(6cm); // ADJUST

pair r = (0, 0);
pair t = dir(-55);
pair s = dir(-55 - 70);
pair p = dir(-55 + 180);
real qr = Sin(70) / Sin(40);

pair q = qr * dir(-55 - 70 + 180);

draw(r--p--q--s--t--r);

label("$R$", r, 2 * E);
label("$T$", t, SE);
label("$S$", s, SW);
label("$P$", p, W);
label("$Q$", q, E);

label("$x^\circ$", t, W + NW + NW);
label("$40^\circ$", q, 4 * SW);

add(pathticks(p--q, spacing=1, s=4));
add(pathticks(q--r, spacing=1, s=4));
add(pathticks(r--s, 2, spacing=1, s=4));
add(pathticks(r--t, 2, spacing=1, s=4));
[/asy]

Solution:
answer:  Since $PRT$ and $QRS$ are straight lines, we know that $\angle PQR + \angle QRS = 180^\circ$. Therefore, we have

$$x^\circ + 40^\circ = 180^\circ.$$

Solving for $x$, we get

$$x = 180^\circ - 40^\circ = 140^\circ.$$

Thus, the value of $x$ is $\boxed{140}$.
====================

