Problem:
In the diagram, $K$, $O$ and $M$ are the centers of the three semi-circles. Also, $OC = 32$ and $CB = 36$. [asy]
pair A, K, O, C, M, B, X, Y, Z;
O=(0,0);
C=(32,0);
M=(50,0);
B=(68,0);
A=(-68,0);
K=(A+C)/2;
X=(0,68);
Y=(-18,50);
Z=(50,18);
path nom, bigc, middlec, smallc;
nom=A--B--(100,100)--(-100,100)--cycle;
bigc=A..X..B--cycle;
middlec=A..Y..C--cycle;
smallc=C..Z..B--cycle;
fill(bigc, gray(.5));
fill(middlec, white);
fill(smallc, white);
draw(smallc);
draw(middlec);
draw(bigc);
draw(A--B);
label("A", A, S);
label("K", K, S);
label("O", O, S);
label("M", M, S);
label("C", C, S);
label("B", B, S);
dot(K);
dot(O);
dot(M);
[/asy] What is the area of the shaded region?

Solution:
answer:  The area of the shaded region is the area of the largest semi-circle minus the areas of the smaller semi-circles. The area of the largest semi-circle is $\frac{1}{2}\pi(36^2) = 648\pi$. The area of the middle semi-circle is $\frac{1}{2}\pi(16^2) = 32\pi$ and the area of the small semi-circle is $\frac{1}{2}\pi(16^2) = 32\pi$. Therefore, the area of the shaded region is $648\pi - 32\pi - 32\pi = \boxed{584\pi}$.
