Problem:
A semi-circle of radius 8 cm, rocks back and forth along a line.  The distance between the line on which the semi-circle sits and the line above is 12 cm.  As it rocks without slipping, the semi-circle touches the line above at two points.  (When the semi-circle hits the line above, it immediately rocks back in the other direction.)  What is the distance between these two points, in millimetres, rounded off to the nearest whole number? [asy]

draw((-15, -8)--(15, -8));draw((-15, 4)--(15, 4));draw((-8, 0)--(8, 0){down}..{up}(-8, 0));

[/asy] (Note: After finding the exact value of the desired distance, you may find a calculator useful to round this value off to the nearest whole number.)

Solution:
answer:  To find the distance between the two points where the semi-circle touches the line above, we need to consider the geometry of the semi-circle and the distance between the two lines.

The semi-circle has a radius of 8 cm, and the distance between the line on which the semi-circle sits and the line above is 12 cm. This means that the semi-circle rocks back and forth along the line, touching the line above at two points.

The distance between the two points where the semi-circle touches the line above is the sum of the radius of the semi-circle and the distance between the two lines. Therefore, the distance between the two points is:

\[ 8 \text{ cm} + 12 \text{ cm} = 20 \text{ cm} \]

Since the problem asks for the distance in millimeters and rounded off to the nearest whole number, we convert 20 cm to millimeters:

\[ 20 \text{ cm} = 200 \text{ mm} \]

Rounding 200 mm to the nearest whole number, we get:

\[ \boxed{200 \text{ mm}} \]
