Problem:
Find $AX$ in the diagram if $CX$ bisects $\angle ACB$. [asy]
import markers;

real t=1-.32;
pair A=(-4.07,0);
pair B=(40.008,0);
pair C=(0,20.616);
pair X=t*A+(1-t)*B;

draw(C--A--B--C--X);

label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,E);
label("$C$",C,N);
label("$X$",X,S);

//markangle(n=1,radius=15,A,C,X,marker(markinterval(stickframe(n=1),true)));
//markangle(n=1,radius=15,X,C,B,marker(markinterval(stickframe(n=1),true)));

label("$30$",.5*(B+X),S);
label("$45$",.5*(B+C),NE);
label("$21$",.5*(A+C),NW);
[/asy]

Solution:
answer:  Since $CX$ bisects $\angle ACB$, we have $\angle ACX = \angle BCX = 15^\circ$.

In $\triangle ACX$, we can use the Law of Cosines to find $AX$:

\begin{align*}
AX^2 &= AC^2 + CX^2 - 2 \cdot AC \cdot CX \cdot \cos(\angle ACX) \\
&= 21^2 + 30^2 - 2 \cdot 21 \cdot 30 \cdot \cos(15^\circ) \\
&= 441 + 900 - 630 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \\
&= 1341 - 315\sqrt{2} \\
\end{align*}

Taking the square root of both sides, we get:

$$AX = \sqrt{1341 - 315\sqrt{2}}.$$

Therefore, the length of $AX$ is $\boxed{\sqrt{1341 - 315\sqrt{2}}}$.
