\begin{align*}
&\frac{a^3}{(a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(a - e)} + \frac{b^3}{(b - a)(b - c)(b - d)(b - e)} \\
&\quad + \frac{c^3}{(c - a)(c - b)(c - d)(c - e)} + \frac{d^3}{(d - a)(d - b)(d - c)(d - e)} \\
&\quad + \frac{e^3}{(e - a)(e - b)(e - c)(e - d)}.
\end{align*}

Solution:
answer:  We can rewrite the given expression as

$$\frac{a^3}{(a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(a - e)} + \frac{b^3}{(b - a)(b - c)(b - d)(b - e)} + \frac{c^3}{(c - a)(c - b)(c - d)(c - e)} + \frac{d^3}{(d - a)(d - b)(d - c)(d - e)} + \frac{e^3}{(e - a)(e - b)(e - c)(e - d)}.$$

We can simplify this expression by using the fact that the roots of the polynomial $x^5 + 7x^4 - 2 = 0$ are distinct. Specifically, we can use the fact that the sum of the roots of the polynomial is zero, and the product of the roots of the polynomial taken one at a time is $-2$.

Using these facts, we can simplify the expression to

$$\boxed{0}.$$
