Problem:
A block of cheese in the shape of a rectangular solid measures $10$ cm by $13$ cm by $14$ cm. Ten slices are cut from the cheese. Each slice has a width of $1$ cm and is cut parallel to one face of the cheese. The individual slices are not necessarily parallel to each other. What is the maximum possible volume in cubic cm of the remaining block of cheese after ten slices have been cut off?

Solution:
answer:  To find the maximum possible volume of the remaining block of cheese after ten slices have been cut off, we need to consider the dimensions of the cheese and the effect of the slices.

The original dimensions of the cheese are 10 cm by 13 cm by 14 cm. Each slice has a width of 1 cm and is cut parallel to one face of the cheese. Therefore, the dimensions of the remaining block after ten slices will be:
- Length: 10 cm - 10 slices = 0 cm
- Width: 13 cm - 10 slices = 3 cm
- Height: 14 cm - 10 slices = 4 cm

The volume of the remaining block is the product of its dimensions:
\[
\text{Volume} = 0 \text{ cm} \times 3 \text{ cm} \times 4 \text{ cm} = 0 \text{ cubic cm}
\]

Thus, the maximum possible volume of the remaining block of cheese after ten slices have been cut off is \(\boxed{0}\) cubic cm.
