Problem:
Find the remainder when the polynomial $x^{18}+x^{13}+x^7+x^4+x$ is divided by $x^3-x$.

Solution:
answer:  We can use polynomial division to find the remainder. We start by dividing $x^{18}$ by $x^3$, which gives $x^{15}$. We then multiply $x^{15}$ by $x^3-x$ to get $x^{18}-x^{15}$. We subtract this from the original polynomial to get $x^{13}+x^7+x^4+x$. We then divide $x^{13}$ by $x^3$, which gives $x^{10}$. We multiply $x^{10}$ by $x^3-x$ to get $x^{13}-x^{10}$. We subtract this from the previous polynomial to get $x^7+x^4+x$. We then divide $x^7$ by $x^3$, which gives $x^4$. We multiply $x^4$ by $x^3-x$ to get $x^7-x^4$. We subtract this from the previous polynomial to get $x$. Therefore, the remainder is $\boxed{x}$.
