Problem:
Find the number of real solutions $(x,y,z,w)$ of the simultaneous equations
\begin{align*}
2y &= x + \frac{17}{x}, \\
2z &= y + \frac{17}{y}, \\
2w &= z + \frac{17}{z}, \\
2x &= w + \frac{17}{w}.
\end{align*}

Solution:
answer:  We can rewrite each equation as:
\begin{align*}
2y &= x + \frac{17}{x}, \\
2z &= y + \frac{17}{y}, \\
2w &= z + \frac{17}{z}, \\
2x &= w + \frac{17}{w}.
\end{align*}

Notice that each equation is a permutation of the first equation. This means that the system of equations is dependent and has infinitely many solutions.

Therefore, the number of real solutions is $\boxed{\infty}$.
