Problem:
The sign function $\text{sgn}(x)$ is defined by \[\text{sgn}(x) = \left\{ \begin{aligned} -1 \quad &\text{if } x < 0 \\ 0 \quad &\text{if } x = 0 \\ 1 \quad &\text{if } x > 0. \end{aligned} \right. \]Compute $\sum_{n=-10}^{20} \text{sgn}(n).$

Solution:
answer:  The sign function $\text{sgn}(x)$ is defined as:
\[
\text{sgn}(x) = \left\{ \begin{aligned}
-1 \quad &\text{if } x < 0 \\
0 \quad &\text{if } x = 0 \\
1 \quad &\text{if } x > 0.
\end{aligned} \right.
\]

We need to compute the sum $n=-10$ to $n=20$:
\[
\sum_{n=-10}^{20} \text{sgn}(n)
\]

Given the definition of the sign function, we can rewrite the sum as follows:
\[
\sum_{n=-10}^{20} \text{sgn}(n) = \sum_{n=-10}^{20} 1
\]

This is a simple sum of 31 terms, each equal to 1:
\[
\sum_{n=-10}^{20} 1 = 31
\]

Thus, the sum is:
\[
\boxed{31}
\]
====================

