Problem:
Let $p(x)$ be a polynomial of degree 100 such that
\begin{align*}
p(1) &= 1, \\
p(2) &= 2, \\
p(3) &= 3, \\
&\dots, \\
p(99) &= 99, \\
p(100) &= 100, \\
p(101) &= 102.
\end{align*}Find $p(102).$

Solution:
answer:  We can write the polynomial $p(x)$ as

$$p(x) = (x-1)(x-2)(x-3)\cdots(x-100)(x-101) + c,$$

where $p(x)$ is a polynomial of degree 100 and $c$ is a constant. We know that $p(101) = 102$, so

$$p(101) = (101-1)(101-2)(101-3)\cdots(101-100)(101-101) + c = 102.$$

Simplifying, we get

$$c = 102.$$

Therefore, $p(102) = (102-1)(102-2)(102-3)\cdots(102-100)(102-101) + 102 = \boxed{102}$.
