Problem:
Use synthetic division to divide $x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9$ by $x^2-3x+2$ and find the remainder.

Solution:
answer:  First, we factor the divisor $x^2-3x+2$ as $(x-1)(x-2)$. Then, we use synthetic division to divide $x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9$ by $(x-1)(x-2)$.

\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
1 & 1 & -3 & 4 & 11 & -9 \\
\hline
2 & 1 & -2 & 2 & 7 & -1 \\
\end{array}

The quotient is $x^3-2x^2+2x+7$ and the remainder is $-1$.

\boxed{-1}
