Problem:
The set of real numbers $x$ for which
$$\dfrac{1}{x-2009}+\dfrac{1}{x-2010}+\dfrac{1}{x-2011}\ge 1$$is the union of intervals of the form $a<x\le b$. What is the sum of the lengths of these intervals?

Solution:
answer:  The given inequality can be rewritten as

$$\dfrac{1}{x-2009}+\dfrac{1}{x-2010}+\dfrac{1}{x-2011}\ge 1.$$

This inequality is satisfied when $x$ is not equal to $2009$, $2010$, or $2011$. Therefore, the solution set is the union of the intervals $(2009, 2010)$, $(2010, 2011)$, and $(2011,2011)$.

The length of each interval is $1$, so the sum of the lengths of these intervals is

$$1 + 1 + 1 = \boxed{3}.$$
