Problem:
The function defined by
\[f(x) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
x + k & \text{if $x < 4$}, \\
2x - 3 & \text{if $x \ge 4$}
\end{array}
\right.\]has an inverse, and the inverse is defined for all real numbers.  Enter all possible values of $k,$ separated by commas.

Solution:
answer:  For the function to have an inverse, it must be one-to-one. This means that the function must be strictly increasing or strictly decreasing on its domain.

For $x < 4$, the function $f(x) = x + k$ is strictly increasing if $k > 1$.

For $x \ge 4$, the function $f(x) = 2x - 3$ is strictly increasing if $k > 1$.

Therefore, for the function to be one-to-one, $k > 1$ for both cases.

Thus, the possible values of $k$ are $\boxed{(1,\infty)}$.
