Problem:
Let
\[f(x) = \frac{-px - 3}{-qx + 3},\]and let $g(x)$ be the inverse of $f(x).$  If $(7,-22)$ lies on both of the graphs of $y = f(x)$ and $y = g(x),$ then find $p + q.$

Solution:
answer:  Since $(7,-22)$ lies on both the graphs of $y = f(x)$ and $y = g(x),$ we have

\[f(7) = g(-22).\]

Substituting $x = 7$ into $f(x),$ we get

\[f(7) = \frac{-7p - 3}{-7q + 3} = -22.\]

Since $g(x)$ is the inverse of $f(x),$ we also have

\[g(-22) = \frac{-22p - 3}{-22q + 3} = -22.\]

Equating the two expressions, we get

\[\frac{-7p - 3}{-7q + 3} = \frac{-22p - 3}{-22q + 3}.\]

Simplifying, we find we have

\[p + q = \boxed{0}.\]
