Problem:
Find all $x$ such that $\frac{1}{x-1}-\frac{1}{x-7}>1$. Express your answer in interval notation.

Solution:
answer:  We can rewrite the inequality as

$$\frac{x-7-x-1}{(x-1)(x-7)}>1.$$Simplifying the numerator, we get

$$\frac{-6}{(x-1)(x-7)}>1.$$This inequality is true when the numerator is negative and the denominator is positive. Therefore, we have

$$x-1<0 \text{ and } x-7>0.$$Solving these inequalities, we get

$$1<x<7.$$Therefore, the solution to the inequality is $\boxed{(1,7)}$.
