Problem:
The sum of two odd functions is always odd, even, or neither?

Enter "odd", "even", or "neither".

Solution:
answer:  The sum of two odd functions is always odd.

To see why, consider two odd functions \( f(x) \) and \( g(x) \). By definition, an odd function satisfies the property \( f(-x) = -f(x) \) and \( g(-x) = -g(x) \).

Now, consider the sum \( h(x) + g(x) \):

\[ h(-x) = f(-x) + g(-x) = -f(x) + (-g(x)) = -h(x) \]

This shows that \( h(x) \) satisfies the property \( h(-x) = -h(x) \), which is the definition of an odd function.

Therefore, the sum of two odd functions is \(\boxed{odd}\).
====================

