Problem:
The graph of the rational function $\frac{q(x)}{x^6-4x^5+3x^3+x^2-1} $ has a horizontal asymptote. What is the largest possible degree of $q(x)$?

Solution:
answer:  For a rational function to have a horizontal asymptote, the degree of the numerator must be less than or equal to the degree of the denominator. The degree of the denominator is 6, so the largest possible degree of $q(x)$ is $\boxed{6}$.
