Problem:
Assume $0 < r < 3$.  Below are five equations for $x$.  Which equation has the largest solution $x$?

$\textbf{(A)}\ 3(1 + r)^x = 7\qquad \textbf{(B)}\ 3(1 + r/10)^x = 7\qquad \textbf{(C)}\ 3(1 + 2r)^x = 7$ $\textbf{(D)}\ 3(1 + \sqrt {r})^x = 7\qquad \textbf{(E)}\ 3(1 + 1/r)^x = 7$

Solution:
answer:  We can rewrite the equations as follows:

\begin{align*}
3(1 + r)^x &= 7 \qquad \Rightarrow\qquad (1 + r)^x = \frac{7}{3}, \\
3(1 + r/10)^x &= 7 \qquad \Rightarrow\qquad (1 + r/10)^x = \frac{7}{3}, \\
3(1 + 2r)^x &= 7 \qquad \Rightarrow\qquad (1 + 2r)^x = \frac{7}{3}, \\
3(1 + \sqrt{r})^x &= 7 \qquad \Rightarrow\qquad (1 + \sqrt{r})^x = \frac{7}{3}, \\
3(1 + 1/r)^x &= 7 \qquad \Rightarrow\qquad (1 + 1/r)^x = \frac{7}{3}.
\end{align*}

Since $0 < r < 3$, we know that $1 + r < 4$. Therefore, the equation with the largest solution $x$ is $\boxed{A}$.
