Problem:
Below is the graph of an ellipse. (Assume that tick marks are placed every $1$ unit along the axes.)
[asy]
size(8cm);

int x, y;
for (y = -10; y <= 2; ++y) {
  draw((-0.6,y/3)--(2.4,y/3),gray(0.7));
}
for (x = -1; x <= 7; ++x) {
  draw((x/3,-3.5)--(x/3,1),gray(0.7));
}

draw(shift((1,-2))*xscale(2/3)*Circle((0,0),1));
draw((0,-3.5)--(0,1),EndArrow);
draw((-.6,0)--(2.4,0),EndArrow);
//for (real i=-1/3; i<=2; i += 1/3)
	//draw((i,0.1)--(i,-0.1));
//for (real i=-3-1/3; i<.9; i += 1/3)
	//draw((-0.1,i)--(0.1,i));
[/asy]
Suppose an equation for the ellipse is \[\frac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} + \frac{(y-k)^2}{b^2} = 1,\]where $a, b, h, k$ are constants and $a, b > 0.$ Compute $a+k.$

Solution:
answer:  The center of the ellipse is at $(1,-2)$, so $h=1$ and $k=-2$. The semi-major axis is $a=2$ and the semi-minor axis is $b=1$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is \[\frac{(x-1)^2}{2^2} + \frac{(y+2)^2}{1^2} = 1,\]which simplifies to \[\frac{(x-1)^2}{4} + \frac{(y+2)^2}{1} = 1.\]Thus, $a+k=2+(-2)=\boxed{0}$.
