Problem:
Find the units digit of the following:  \begin{align*}
& (972 - 268)(973 - 267)(974 - 266) \\
&\qquad+ (968 - 272)(967 - 273)(966 - 274) - (999 - 222)^3
\end{align*}

Solution:
answer:  First, we find the units digit of each term:

\begin{align*}
972 - 268 &= 4 \\
973 - 267 &= 6 \\
974 - 266 &= 8 \\
968 - 272 &= 6 \\
967 - 273 &= 4 \\
966 - 274 &= 2 \\
999 - 222 &= 7 \\
\end{align*}

Next, we calculate the units digit of each product:

\begin{align*}
4 \cdot 6 \cdot 8 &= 96 \\
6 \cdot 4 \cdot 2 &= 48 \\
7^3 &= 343 \\
\end{align*}

Now, we find the units digit of the sum:

\begin{align*}
96 + 48 - 343 &= 11 \\
\end{align*}

Therefore, the units digit of the given expression is $\boxed{1}$.
