Problem:
What is the last nonzero digit to the right of the decimal point in the decimal expansion of $\frac{137}{500}$?

Solution:
answer:  To find the last nonzero digit to the right of the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \(\frac{137}{500}\), we first perform the division \(137 \div 500\).

\[
137 \div 500 = 0.274
\]

The decimal expansion of \(\frac{137}{500}\) is \(0.274\). The last nonzero digit to the right of the decimal point is \(4\).

Therefore, the last nonzero digit to the right of the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \(\frac{137}{500}\) is \(\boxed{4}\).
