Problem:
If $n>1$ is an integer, the notation $a\equiv b\pmod{n}$ means that $(a-b)$ is a multiple of $n$. Find the sum of all possible values of $n$ such that both of the following are true: $171\equiv80\pmod{n}$ and $468\equiv13\pmod{n}$.

Solution:
answer:  We have that $171\equiv80\pmod{n}$ means $171-80\pmod{n}$, which is $91\pmod{n}$. Similarly, $468\equiv13\pmod{n}$ means $468-13\pmod{n}$, which is $455\pmod{n}$. Therefore, $n$ must be a divisor of both $91$ and $455$. The greatest common divisor of $91$ and $455$ is $91$. Therefore, $n$ must be a divisor of $91$. The divisors of $91$ are $1, 7, 13,$ and $91$. The sum of all possible values of $n$ is $1+7+13+91=\boxed{112}$.
