Final Answer: The final answer is $-\frac{2}{3}$. I hope it is correct.

Problem:
Determine the number of integers $x$ such that $0\le x< 12$ and $x$ satisfies the following system of equations: \begin{align*}
&x-1\equiv 1-x\pmod {12},\\
&x-2\equiv 2-x\pmod{12}.
\end{align*}

Solution:
answer:  First, we simplify each congruence:
\begin{align*}
&x-1\equiv 1-x\pmod{12} \Rightarrow 2x\equiv 2\pmod{12} \Rightarrow x\equiv 1\pmod{6},\\
&x-2\equiv 2-x\pmod{12} \Rightarrow 2x\equiv 4\pmod{12} \Rightarrow x\equiv 2\pmod{6}.
\end{align*}

Next, we find the common solutions to these congruences within the range $0\le x< 12$:
\begin{align*}
&x\equiv 1\pmod{6} \Rightarrow x = 1, 7, 13,\\
&x\equiv 2\pmod{6} \Rightarrow x = 2, 8, 10.
\end{align*}

The common solutions are $x = 2$ and $x = 8$. Therefore, there are $\boxed{2}$ integers $x$ that satisfy the system of equations.
