A book with 50 pages numbered 1 through 50 has its pages renumbered in reverse, from 50 to 1. For how many pages do both sets of page numbers share the same ones digit?

Solution:
answer:  To determine how many pages in both sets of page numbers share the same ones digit, we need to analyze the ones digits of the page numbers in both sets.

First, consider the way the ones digits of the page numbers change when the pages are reversed. The ones digit of a number is the same as the ones digit of the number minus 10. For example, the ones digit of 11 is the same as the ones digit of 1, which is 1.

Therefore, the ones digits of the page numbers in the original set (1 to 50) will be the same as the ones digits of the page numbers in the reversed set (50 to 1).

Since there are 50 pages in each set, and the ones digits of the page numbers in both sets are the same, all 50 pages in both sets will share the same ones digit.

Thus, the number of pages where both sets of page numbers share the same ones digit is \(\boxed{50}\).
