Problem:
Jim and Martha are standing together at the corner of a rectangular field. Jim walks diagonally across the field. Martha gets to the same location by walking along its length and width. The field is 300 feet wide and 400 feet long. How many feet less than Martha does Jim walk?

Solution:
answer:  Jim walks diagonally across the field, so the distance he walks is the length of the diagonal of the rectangle. We can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of the diagonal:

$$\text{Diagonal} = \sqrt{\text{length}^2 + \text{width}^2} = \sqrt{400^2 + 300^2} = \sqrt{160000 + 90000} = \sqrt{250000} = 500 \text{ feet}$$

Martha walks along the length and width of the field, so the distance she walks is the sum of the length and width:

$$\text{Distance} = \text{length} + \text{width} = 400 + 300 = 700 \text{ feet}$$

The difference in distance between Jim and Martha is:

$$\Delta \ Distance = 700 - 500 = 200 \text{ feet}$$

Therefore, Jim walks $\boxed{200}$ feet less than Martha.
====================

