Problem:
Three circular arcs of radius 5 units bound the region shown. Arcs $AB$ and $AD$ are quarter-circles, and arc $BCD$ is a semicircle. What is the area, in square units, of the region? [asy]
/* AMC8 2000 #19 Problem */
draw((0,0)..(1,1)..(2,0));
draw((0,0)..(.7,-.3)..(1,-1));
draw((1,-1)..(1.3, -0.3)..(2,0));
label("$A$", (1,-1), SW);
label("$B$", (0,0), W);
label("$C$", (1,1),N);
label("$D$", (2,0),E);
[/asy]

Solution:
answer:  The area of a full circle with radius 5 is $\pi(5^2)=25\pi$ square units. The area of a quarter-circle is $\frac{1}{4}(25\pi)=6.25\pi$ square units. The area of a semicircle is $\frac{1}{2}(25\pi)=12.5\pi$ square units. Therefore, the area of the region is $6.25\pi+12.5\pi=\boxed{18.75\pi}$ square units.
