Problem:
Each good worker can paint my new house alone in 12 hours.  Each bad worker can paint my house alone in 36 hours.  I need my house painted in 3 hours.  If I can only find 3 good workers, how many bad workers must I also find in order to have my house painted on time?

Solution:
answer:  Each good worker can paint the house in 12 hours, so 3 good workers can paint the house in $3 \times 12 = 36$ hours.

We need the house painted in 3 hours, so we need to find how many bad workers are required to complete the remaining $36 - 3 = 33$ hours.

Each bad worker can paint the house in 36 hours, so we need $\frac{33}{36} = \frac{11}{12}$ bad workers.

Therefore, we need $\boxed{\frac{11}{12}}$ bad workers.
