Problem:
In the diagram, the three concentric circles have radii of $4,$ $6,$ and $7.$ Three regions are labeled $X,$ $Y,$ or $Z$ below. Of these three regions, what is the difference between the area of the region with the greatest area and the area of the region with the smallest area? Express your answer in exact form.

[asy]
import graph;
filldraw(circle((0,0),7), lightgray, black+linewidth(1));
filldraw(circle((0,0),6), gray, black+linewidth(1));
filldraw(circle((0,0),4), white, black+linewidth(1));
dot((0,0));
label("$X$",(2,0));
label("$Y$",(5,0));
label("$Z$",(6.5,0));
[/asy]

Solution:
answer:  The area of the largest circle is $\pi(7^2) = 49\pi$. The area of the medium circle is $\pi(6^2) = 36\pi$. The area of the smallest circle is $\pi(4^2) = 16\pi$. The area of the region $X$ is $49\pi - 16\pi = 33\pi$. The area of the region $Y$ is $36\pi - 16\pi = 20\pi$. The area of the region $Z$ is $49\pi - 36\pi = 13\pi$. The difference between the area of the region with the greatest area and the area of the region with the smallest area is $33\pi - 13\pi = \boxed{20\pi}$.
====================

