Problem:
Joe was in charge of lights for a dance. The red light blinks every two seconds, the yellow light every three seconds, and the blue light every five seconds. If we include the very beginning and very end of the dance, how many times during a seven minute dance will all the lights come on at the same time? (Assume that all three lights blink simultaneously at the very beginning of the dance.)

Solution:
answer:  To determine how many times all three lights will come on at the same time during a seven-minute dance, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of their blinking intervals. The blinking intervals are 2 seconds, 3 seconds, and 5 seconds.

First, we find the LCM of 2 and 3:
\[
\text{LCM}(2, 3) = 6
\]

Next, we find the LCM of 6 and 5:
\[
\text{LCM}(6, 5) = 30
\]

Thus, the LCM of 2, 3, and 5 is 30 seconds.

Now, we need to determine how many 30-second intervals fit into 7 minutes:
\[
7 \text{ minutes} = 420 \text{ seconds}
\]
\[
\frac{420 \text{ seconds}}{30 \text{ seconds/interval}} = 14 \text{ intervals}
\]

Therefore, all three lights will come on at the same time \boxed{14} times during the seven-minute dance.
