Problem:
In regular pentagon $FGHIJ$, extending the sides of the pentagon, as shown, forms a star. What is the measure of angle $A$ in the figure?

[asy]
draw((-42.4,30.8)--(-10,30.8)--(0,63.2)--(10,30.8)--(42.4,30.8)--(16.2,11.8)--(24.9,-18.1)--(0,0)--(-24.9,-18.1)--(-16.2,11.8)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((-10,30.8)--(10,30.8)--(16.2,11.8)--(0,0)--(-16.2,11.8)--cycle,linewidth(1));
label("$A$",(-42.4,30.8),W);
label("$F$",(-10,30.8),NW);
dot((-10,30.8));
label("$G$",(10,30.8),NE);
dot((10,30.8));
label("$H$",(16.2,11.8),E);
dot((16.2,11.8));
label("$I$",(0,0),S);
dot((0,0));
label("$J$",(-16.2,11.8),WSW);
dot((-16.2,11.8));
[/asy]

Solution:
answer:  To find the measure of angle \( A \) in the figure, we need to understand the properties of the regular pentagon and the star formed by extending its sides.

A regular pentagon has five sides and five equal angles. The interior angle of a regular pentagon is \( 108^\circ \).

When we extend the sides of the pentagon to form a star, the angle \( A \) is the angle formed by two sides of the pentagon at the vertex. This angle is the same as the interior angle of the pentagon, which is \( 108^\circ \).

Therefore, the measure of angle \( A \) is \(\boxed{108^\circ}\).
