Problem:
The triangles whose vertices are $\overrightarrow{A},$ $\overrightarrow{B},$ and $\overrightarrow{C}$ has area 12.  Find the area of the triangle whose vertices are $-\overrightarrow{A} + \overrightarrow{B} + \overrightarrow{C},$ $\overrightarrow{A} - \overrightarrow{B} + \overrightarrow{C},$ and $\overrightarrow{A} + \overrightarrow{B} - \overrightarrow{C}.$

Solution:
answer:  The area of a triangle with vertices $\overrightarrow{A}, \overrightarrow{B}, \overrightarrow{C}$ is given by the magnitude of the scalar triple product of these vectors. The area of the triangle with vertices $-\overrightarrow{A} + \overrightarrow{B} + \overrightarrow{C}, \overrightarrow{A} - \overrightarrow{B} + \overrightarrow{C}, \overrightarrow{A} + \overrightarrow{B} - \overrightarrow{C}$ is given by the magnitude of the scalar triple product of these vectors.

Since the scalar triple product is a linear operation, the area of the new triangle is the same as the area of the original triangle. Therefore, the area of the new triangle is $\boxed{12}$.
====================

