Problem:
Simplify
\[4 \sin x \sin (60^\circ - x) \sin (60^\circ + x).\]The answer will be a trigonometric function of some simple function of $x,$ like "$\cos (2x)$" or "$\sin (x^3)$".

Solution:
answer:  We can use the product-to-sum identities to simplify the expression. Recall that

\[4 \sin A \sin B \sin C = \cos(A + B - C).\]

In this case, we have

\[4 \sin x \sin (60^\circ - x) \sin (60^\circ + x) = \cos(x + (60^\circ - x) - (60^\circ + x)) = \cos(00^\circ) = \cos(0^\circ) = 1.\]

Therefore, the simplified expression is

\[\boxed{1}.\]
