Problem:
Find the cross product of $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 4 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}.$

Solution:
answer:  The cross product of two vectors $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 4 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}$ is given by:

$$\begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 & -5 \end{vmatrix} = \mathbf{i}((-1)(-5) - (2)(4)) - \mathbf{j}(1)(-5) - (2)(3)) + \mathbf{k}(1)(4) - (-1)(3))$$

$$= \mathbf{i}(5 - 8) - \mathbf{j}(-5 - 6) + \mathbf{k}(4 + 3)$$

$$= \mathbf{i}(-3) - \mathbf{j}(-11) + \mathbf{k}(7)$$

$$= \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 11 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$$

Therefore, the cross product of the two vectors is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 11 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}}$.
