Problem:
Find all possible values of the determinant of
\[\begin{pmatrix} \sec^2 x & 1 & 1 \\ \cos^2 x & \cos^2 x & \csc^2 x \\ 1 & \cos^2 x & \cot^2 x \end{pmatrix},\]as $x$ ranges over all real numbers (where the determinant is defined).

Solution:
answer:  The determinant of the given matrix is
\begin{align*}
(\sec^2 x)(\cos^2 x)(\cot^2 x) - (1)(\cos^2 x)(\csc^2 x) + (1)(\cos^2 x)(\cot^2 x) \\
&= (\sec^2 x)(\cos^2 x)(\cot^2 x) - (\cos^2 x)(\csc^2 x) + (\cos^2 x)(\cot^2 x) \\
&= (\sec^2 x)(\cos^2 x)(\cot^2 x) - (\cos^2 x)(\csc^2 x) + (\cos^2 x)(\cot^2 x) \\
&= (\sec^2 x)(\cos^2 x)(\cot^2 x) - (\cos^2 x)(\csc^2 x) + (\cos^2 x)(\cot^2 x) \\
&= 0.
\end{align*}
Therefore, the determinant of the matrix is $\boxed{0}$ for all real numbers $x$.
