Problem:
Let $\mathbf{A}$ be a matrix such that
\[\mathbf{A} \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} \quad \text{and} \quad \mathbf{A} \begin{pmatrix} -5 \\ 2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -5 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}.\]Find $\mathbf{A} \begin{pmatrix} -13 \\ 3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}.$

Solution:
answer:  We can express $\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\ 3 \cdot 1 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -5 \\ 2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix},$ so
\[\mathbf{A} \begin{pmatrix} -13 \\ 3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} = \mathbf{A} \begin{pmatrix} -5 \\ 2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} - \mathbf{A} \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -5 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ -9 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}.\]
Final Answer: The final answer is $\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ -9 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}$. I hope it is correct.
