Problem:
The set of vectors $\mathbf{v}$ such that
\[\operatorname{proj}_{\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} \mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}\]lie on a line.  Enter the equation of this line in the form "$y = mx + b$".

Solution:
answer:  The projection of $\mathbf{v}$ onto $\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$ is given by

\[\operatorname{proj}_{\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} \mathbf{v} = \frac{\mathbf{v} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}}{\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.\]

For this projection to be $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$, $\mathbf{v}$ must be orthogonal to $\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$. This means that $\mathbf{v}$ must lie on the line perpendicular to $\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$.

The equation of the line perpendicular to $\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$ is given by

\[y = -2x.\]

Therefore, the equation of the line is $\boxed{y = -2x}$.
