Problem:
Find the vector $\mathbf{v}$ such that
\[\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 & -1 \\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\ 4 & 0 & -2 \end{pmatrix} \mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 27 \\ -14 \end{pmatrix}.\]

Solution:
answer:  We can solve the system of equations by row reducing the augmented matrix.

First, we subtract 2 times the first row from the second row:

\[\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 & -1 \\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\ 4 & 0 & -2 \end{pmatrix} \rightarrow \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 & -1 \\ 0 & 0 & 7 \\ 0 & 0 & -4 \end{pmatrix}.\]

Next, we subtract 2 times the first row from the third row:

\[\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 & -1 \\ 0 & 0 & 7 \\ 0 & 0 & -4 \end{pmatrix} \rightarrow \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 & -1 \\ 0 & 0 & 7 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}.\]

Now, we can see that the system of equations is inconsistent, so there is no solution.

Therefore, the answer is $\boxed{\text{No solution}}.$
