Problem:
Given that
\begin{align*}
\cos x + \cos y + \cos z &= 0, \\
\sin x + \sin y + \sin z &= 0,
\end{align*}find
\begin{align*}
&\tan^2 x + \tan^2 y + \tan^2 z - (\tan^2 x \tan^2 y + \tan^2 x \tan^2 z + \tan^2 y \tan^2 z) \\
&\quad  - 3 \tan^2 x \tan^2 y \tan^2 z.
\end{align*}

Solution:
answer:  We can rewrite the given equations as:
\begin{align*}
\cos x + \cos y + \cos z &= 0, \\
\sin x + \sin y + \sin z &= 0.
\end{align*}
Using the identity $\sin^2 x + \cos^2 x = \sec^2 x$, we have:
\begin{align*}
\sin^2 x + \cos^2 x + \sin^2 y + \cos^2 y + \sin^2 z + \cos^2 z &= 0, \\
\cos^2 x + \cos^2 y + \cos^2 z + \sin^2 x + \sin^2 y + \sin^2 z &= 0.
\end{align*}
Subtracting the second equation from the first, we get:
\begin{align*}
\sin^2 x + \sin^2 y + \sin^2 z + \cos^2 x + \cos^2 y + \cos^2 z - \sin^2 x - \sin^2 y - \sin^2 z &= 0, \\

