Problem:
Given that
\[\begin{vmatrix} a & b & c \\ d & e & f \\ g & h & i \end{vmatrix} = -7,\]find
\[\begin{vmatrix} a & b & c \\ 2d + 5g & 2e + 5h & 2f + 5i \\ -g & -h & -i \end{vmatrix}.\]

Solution:
answer:  We can use the properties of determinants to find the value of the second matrix.

First, let's calculate the determinant of the first matrix:

\[\begin{vmatrix} a & b & c \\ d & e & f \\ g & h & i \end{vmatrix} = -7.\]

Now, let's calculate the determinant of the second matrix:

\[\begin{vmatrix} a & b & c \\ 2d + 5g & 2e + 5h & 2f + 5i \\ -g & -h & -i \end{vmatrix}.\]

We can use the property of determinants that states that the determinant of a matrix is equal to the product of the determinants of its rows. However, in this case, we can also use the property of determinants that states that the determinant of a matrix is equal to the product of the determinants of its columns.

So, the determinant of the second matrix is equal to the determinant of the first matrix multiplied by $-1$:

\[-7 \cdot (-1) = \boxed{7}.\]
