Final Answer: The final answer is $-\frac{2}{3}$. I hope it is correct.

Problem:
A particle moves so that it is at $\left( 3 \sin \frac{t}{4}, 3 \cos \frac{t}{4} \right)$ at time $t.$  Find the speed of the particle, measured in unit of distance per unit of time.

Solution:
answer:  The speed of the particle is the magnitude of the velocity vector. The velocity vector is given by the derivative of the position vector with respect to time.

The position vector is $\left( 3 \sin \frac{t}{4}, 3 \cos \frac{t}{4} \right)$.

The velocity vector is $\left( \frac{3}{4} \cos \frac{t}{4}, -\frac{3}{4} \sin \frac{t}{4} \right)$.

The magnitude of the velocity vector is $\sqrt{\left( \frac{3}{4} \cos \frac{t}{4} \right)^2 + \left( -\frac{3}{4} \sin \frac{t}{4} \right)^2} = \sqrt{\frac{9}{16} \cos^2 \frac{t}{4} + \frac{9}{16} \sin^2 \frac{t}{4}} = \sqrt{\frac{9}{16} \cdot 1} = \boxed{\frac{3}{4}}$.
====================

