Problem:
The matrices
\[\mathbf{A} = \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.5} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & x \\ y & -\frac{9}{5} \end{pmatrix} \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1} \quad \text{and} \quad \mathbf{B} = \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.5} \begin{pmatrix} \frac{12}{5} & \frac{1}{10} \\ 5 & z \end{pmatrix} \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1}\]satisfy $\mathbf{A} + \mathbf{B} = \mathbf{A} \mathbf{B}.$  Find $x + y + z.$

Solution:
answer:  We have that $\mathbf{A} + \mathbf{B} = \mathbf{A} \mathbf{B}$, which means that the corresponding elements of the matrices must be equal. Therefore, we have the following system of equations:

\begin{align*}
1 + \frac{12}{5} &= x + \frac{1}{10}, \\
y + 5 &= y - \frac{9}{5}, \\
-\frac{9}{5} + z &= -\frac{9}{5} + z.
\end{align*}

Simplifying each equation, we get:

\begin{align*}
\frac{17}{5} &= x + \frac{1}{10}, \\
5 &= 5, \\
z &= \frac{12}{5}.
\end{align*}

Therefore, $x + y + z = \frac{17}{5} + 5 + \frac{12}{5} = \frac{39}{5} = \boxed{7.8}$.
====================

====================
