Problem:
A rotation centered at the origin takes $\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$ to $\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix}.$  Which vector does the rotation take $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$ to?

Solution:
answer:  The rotation centered at the origin takes $\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$ to $\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix}$. This means that the rotation rotates the vector $\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$ to $\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix}$.

To find the vector that the rotation takes $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$ to, we can use the fact that the rotation rotates the vector $\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$ to $\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix}$. This means that the rotation rotates the vector $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$ to $\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}$.

Therefore, the vector that the rotation takes $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$ to is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}}$.
