Problem:
The matrices
\[\begin{pmatrix} 2 & a \\ -3 & -1 \end{pmatrix} \quad \text{and} \quad \begin{pmatrix} -\frac{1}{16} & b \\ \frac{3}{16} & \frac{1}{8} \end{pmatrix}\]are inverses.  Enter the ordered pair $(a,b).$

Solution:
answer:  For the matrices to be inverses, their product must be the identity matrix. Therefore, we have:

\[\begin{pmatrix} 2 & a \\ -3 & -1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} -\frac{1}{16} & b \\ \frac{3}{16} & \frac{1}{8} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}.\]

This gives us the system of equations:

\[2\left(-\frac{1}{16}\right) + a\left(\frac{3}{16}\right) = 1,\]
\[-3\left(-\frac{1}{16}\right) + (-1)\left(\frac{3}{16}\right) = 0.\]

Simplifying these equations, we get:

\[-\frac{1}{8} + \frac{3a}{16} = 1,\]
\[-\frac{3}{16} - \frac{3}{16} = 0.\]

